Newton's third law states: if object A exerts a force on object B, then object B exerts an equal and opposite force on object A. The two forces are equal in magnitude, opposite in direction, and act on different objects.

Everyday example — walking: When you take a step, your foot pushes backward and downward against the ground. This is the action force. The ground simultaneously pushes forward and upward against your foot with an equal force — the reaction force. That forward push from the ground is what moves your body forward. Without it, your foot would simply slide backward (as on ice), and you would not make progress.

Regarding the numeric values from Example 2 (inputs 132, 10, 35 and outputs x=7, y=5): these belong to an unrelated problem domain and are not relevant to stating or applying the third law.
</answer>

