Bayes' theorem (or Bayes' rule) states:

\[
P(A \mid B) = \frac{P(B \mid A) \, P(A)}{P(B)}
\]

It lets you compute the posterior probability \(P(A \mid B)\) — the probability of event A given that B has occurred — when you know the likelihood \(P(B \mid A)\), the prior \(P(A)\), and the marginal probability of the evidence \(P(B)\). This is the foundation of Bayesian inference: updating beliefs about A after observing data B.
</answer>

