A deadlock occurs when two or more threads become permanently blocked, each waiting for a resource that another thread holds. The four necessary conditions, known as the Coffman conditions, are: (1) Mutual Exclusion — at least one resource must be held in a non-shareable mode; (2) Hold and Wait — a thread holds at least one resource while waiting for additional resources held by other threads; (3) No Preemption — resources cannot be forcibly taken from a thread; (4) Circular Wait — there exists a circular chain of threads where each thread holds a resource that the next thread in the chain needs.
</answer>

